{"candidateKey":"con-12|fallacy|Equivocation","kind":"card","definition":"Shifting the meaning of a key word or phrase during the argument, making the reasoning seem stronger than it is.","source":{"moveId":"con-12","side":"con","sectionTitle":"definition, scientific reach, and burden of proof","role":"Reply","currentDescription":"Because “immaterial substance” is allegedly incoherent and an interacting soul should be verifiable, Aron Ra concludes that Hernandez has neither adequately defined nor demonstrated the proposed entity.","lockedProposition":"'Immaterial substance' is alleged to be incoherent, and a real soul should have verifiable implications rather than being insulated from investigation.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"philosophy he said he said that a soul is immaterial substance which is a contradiction in itself that what the words don't even make sense together right there would be a way to verify if we actually had a soul there's lots of different ways we could do that we can't just say that it's mystical magical whimsical and you can't touch it don't look man the man behind the curtain there's a curtain we can look behind it","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: Aron Ra connects definition with empirical burden, arguing that “immaterial substance” needs coherent meaning and that a soul animating a body should yield verifiable implications instead of protection from investigation by definition. Principal limitation: He never shows that immateriality contradicts Hernandez’s sense of substance, while his curtain imagery dismisses the proposal without addressing Hernandez’s definition or concessions about embodied dependence. Live burden: Aron Ra must preserve the demand for testable consequences while defending or abandoning the semantic contradiction claim, then identify an observation that would count against Hernandez’s soul model and explain why."},"presentedReviews":[{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-1","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Calling the phrase contradictory may rest on an unstated physical sense of “substance,” but the excerpt does not visibly shift that word's meaning during an argument; it merely asserts incoherence without support.","context":null},{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-2","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Aron Ra treats “substance” as necessarily material when declaring “immaterial substance” contradictory, without addressing Hernandez’s metaphysical use of the term.","context":"He calls “immaterial substance” self-contradictory by treating substance as material rather than addressing Hernandez’s metaphysical meaning."}]}
{"candidateKey":"con-08|bias|Confirmation bias","kind":"card","definition":"Seeking, interpreting, or remembering evidence in ways that favor an existing view while discounting contrary information.","source":{"moveId":"con-08","side":"con","sectionTitle":"intentionality, truth-bearing thought, and subjective experience","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"Because near-death visions allegedly reproduce prior religious expectations rather than unfamiliar common facts, Aron Ra concludes that their contents are internally generated rather than externally perceived.","lockedProposition":"Near-death visions reproduce subjects' prior religious expectations rather than revealing unfamiliar common facts, indicating internally generated illusions rather than encounters by a separable soul.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"they expected to see because you know Hindus don't see Jesus and Christians don't see Krishna there is never a revelation of spirituality that the subject did not already know and believed in which indicates that mystic visions and near-death experiences are illusions that only accentuate preconceived notions","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: Aron Ra turns expectation dependence into a discriminating prediction: independent encounters with one external realm should sometimes yield shared, unfamiliar information, whereas reports populated by culturally familiar figures and doctrines support internally generated experience. Principal limitation: His universal claim that visions never disclose previously unknown spirituality lacks representative evidence, while culturally conditioned interpretation could shape a genuine unusual encounter without rendering it illusory. Live burden: He must document how strongly and consistently reports match prior commitments, separate immediate experience from later narration and interpretation, and compare internal generation with alternatives predicting common structure alongside cultural variation."},"presentedReviews":[{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-1","decision":"rejected","rationale":"The speaker alleges that experiencers reproduce prior beliefs, but the excerpt does not itself show him selectively seeking, interpreting, or remembering a body of contrary evidence. The assessed defect is an unsupported universal and incomplete comparison, not demonstrated confirmation bias.","context":null}]}
{"candidateKey":"con-08|bias|Scope neglect","kind":"card","definition":"Failing to adjust judgment in proportion to the scale, number, or magnitude of what is being discussed.","source":{"moveId":"con-08","side":"con","sectionTitle":"intentionality, truth-bearing thought, and subjective experience","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"Because near-death visions allegedly reproduce prior religious expectations rather than unfamiliar common facts, Aron Ra concludes that their contents are internally generated rather than externally perceived.","lockedProposition":"Near-death visions reproduce subjects' prior religious expectations rather than revealing unfamiliar common facts, indicating internally generated illusions rather than encounters by a separable soul.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"they expected to see because you know Hindus don't see Jesus and Christians don't see Krishna there is never a revelation of spirituality that the subject did not already know and believed in which indicates that mystic visions and near-death experiences are illusions that only accentuate preconceived notions","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: Aron Ra turns expectation dependence into a discriminating prediction: independent encounters with one external realm should sometimes yield shared, unfamiliar information, whereas reports populated by culturally familiar figures and doctrines support internally generated experience. Principal limitation: His universal claim that visions never disclose previously unknown spirituality lacks representative evidence, while culturally conditioned interpretation could shape a genuine unusual encounter without rendering it illusory. Live burden: He must document how strongly and consistently reports match prior commitments, separate immediate experience from later narration and interpretation, and compare internal generation with alternatives predicting common structure alongside cultural variation."},"presentedReviews":[{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-2","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The categorical claim that spiritual revelations never exceed prior belief expands culturally familiar examples to the entire class without proportional evidential support.","context":"He generalizes from culturally familiar visions to the absolute claim that spiritual revelations never contain previously unknown material."}]}
{"candidateKey":"con-11|fallacy|Appeal to authority","kind":"card","definition":"Relying on a person or institution's status as if it settles the argument, especially when the relevant evidence, expertise, or consensus has not been shown.","source":{"moveId":"con-11","side":"con","sectionTitle":"intentionality, truth-bearing thought, and subjective experience","role":"Reply","currentDescription":"By citing consciousness as neuronal activity generated by a healthy brain, Aron Ra concludes that mental life emanates from neural processes rather than a separate substantial bearer.","lockedProposition":"Aron Ra cites an emergent account on which consciousness is neuronal activity and the mind is generated by the brain.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"far as as Daniel Dennett Daniel Dennett and Patricia Churchland described consciousness as the ebb and flow of neurons inside a healthy human brain meaning that the mind really does emanate from and is generated by the brain and you know this is according to","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: Aron Ra offers a clear competing inference from coordinated neuronal activity to consciousness, citing the “ebb and flow of neurons” to deny Hernandez’s need for an immaterial bearer. Principal limitation: Dennett and Churchland are invoked to assert brain production without explaining neural truth conditions, first-person privacy, nonspatial phenomenal character, or why Hernandez’s comparisons between mental and physical properties fail. Live burden: Aron Ra must supply the missing bridge from neural coordination to intentional content and subjective experience, showing how physical representations become about things and publicly measurable processes constitute privately accessible experience."},"presentedReviews":[{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-1","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Dennett and Churchland's status is used to supply the crucial neuronal-generation conclusion while the excerpt gives no evidential bridge from neural activity to the disputed properties of consciousness.","context":"Aron Ra invokes Dennett and Churchland's description of consciousness to assert that the brain generates the mind without explaining the inference."},{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-2","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Dennett and Churchland are invoked as support for brain-generated consciousness, but their status substitutes for the missing explanation of how neural activity produces the disputed mental properties.","context":"He invokes Dennett and Churchland’s description of consciousness as neuronal activity instead of supplying the disputed explanatory bridge."}]}
{"candidateKey":"con-11|bias|Authority bias","kind":"card","definition":"Giving extra weight to a claim because it comes from a perceived authority, sometimes beyond what the evidence itself supports.","source":{"moveId":"con-11","side":"con","sectionTitle":"intentionality, truth-bearing thought, and subjective experience","role":"Reply","currentDescription":"By citing consciousness as neuronal activity generated by a healthy brain, Aron Ra concludes that mental life emanates from neural processes rather than a separate substantial bearer.","lockedProposition":"Aron Ra cites an emergent account on which consciousness is neuronal activity and the mind is generated by the brain.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"far as as Daniel Dennett Daniel Dennett and Patricia Churchland described consciousness as the ebb and flow of neurons inside a healthy human brain meaning that the mind really does emanate from and is generated by the brain and you know this is according to","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: Aron Ra offers a clear competing inference from coordinated neuronal activity to consciousness, citing the “ebb and flow of neurons” to deny Hernandez’s need for an immaterial bearer. Principal limitation: Dennett and Churchland are invoked to assert brain production without explaining neural truth conditions, first-person privacy, nonspatial phenomenal character, or why Hernandez’s comparisons between mental and physical properties fail. Live burden: Aron Ra must supply the missing bridge from neural coordination to intentional content and subjective experience, showing how physical representations become about things and publicly measurable processes constitute privately accessible experience."},"presentedReviews":[{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-2","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Although named authorities receive argumentative weight, the excerpt does not establish the speaker’s private tendency to overweight authority beyond the visible appeal-to-authority defect.","context":null}]}
{"candidateKey":"pro-08|fallacy|Begging the question","kind":"card","definition":"Assuming, directly or indirectly, the very point that needs to be established, so the conclusion is smuggled into the premises.","source":{"moveId":"pro-08","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"unity, indivisibility, and identity through change","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"Because physical objects admit division whereas personhood allegedly exists only as an indivisible whole, Hernandez concludes that a person is not merely physical but an immaterial soul.","lockedProposition":"Physical aggregates admit division by degrees, but a person is an all-or-nothing subject; therefore a person is not merely a physical aggregate but a soul.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"over a few one is the indivisibility of personhood and it would go like this I'm either a purely physical object or an immaterial soul purely physical objects can be divided in percentages or degrees I cannot be divided or come in percentages or degrees I'm an all-or-nothing kind of thing therefore I'm not a purely physical object therefore I am a soul to","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: Hernandez presents an explicit syllogism connecting physical divisibility, personal indivisibility, and an immaterial subject, so the proposed progression from bodily partition to soul is unusually transparent and directly assessable. Principal limitation: The inference shifts between dividing material components and possessing personhood by degrees, then leaves undefended the claim that only a divisible physical object or an immaterial soul can explain categorical personal unity. Live burden: He must establish indivisibility in the ontologically relevant sense and exclude organized physical systems whose status remains all-or-nothing despite divisible parts, neurological impairment, changing capacities, or distributed processing."},"presentedReviews":[{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-1","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The opening physical-object-or-immaterial-soul premise assumes an exhaustive ontology that already excludes organized physical accounts, which is a central point the conclusion needs established.","context":"Hernandez begins by assuming he must be either a purely physical object or an immaterial soul, excluding rival physical accounts without defense."},{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-2","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The opening physical-object-or-soul dichotomy assumes the exhaustive ontological alternatives needed to infer a soul, thereby embedding the disputed conclusion in the setup.","context":"He begins with “either a purely physical object or an immaterial soul,” assuming the exhaustive alternatives needed for his conclusion."}]}
{"candidateKey":"pro-08|fallacy|Equivocation","kind":"card","definition":"Shifting the meaning of a key word or phrase during the argument, making the reasoning seem stronger than it is.","source":{"moveId":"pro-08","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"unity, indivisibility, and identity through change","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"Because physical objects admit division whereas personhood allegedly exists only as an indivisible whole, Hernandez concludes that a person is not merely physical but an immaterial soul.","lockedProposition":"Physical aggregates admit division by degrees, but a person is an all-or-nothing subject; therefore a person is not merely a physical aggregate but a soul.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"over a few one is the indivisibility of personhood and it would go like this I'm either a purely physical object or an immaterial soul purely physical objects can be divided in percentages or degrees I cannot be divided or come in percentages or degrees I'm an all-or-nothing kind of thing therefore I'm not a purely physical object therefore I am a soul to","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: Hernandez presents an explicit syllogism connecting physical divisibility, personal indivisibility, and an immaterial subject, so the proposed progression from bodily partition to soul is unusually transparent and directly assessable. Principal limitation: The inference shifts between dividing material components and possessing personhood by degrees, then leaves undefended the claim that only a divisible physical object or an immaterial soul can explain categorical personal unity. Live burden: He must establish indivisibility in the ontologically relevant sense and exclude organized physical systems whose status remains all-or-nothing despite divisible parts, neurological impairment, changing capacities, or distributed processing."},"presentedReviews":[{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-1","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The inference moves from physical objects being divisible into material components to persons allegedly coming in percentages or degrees, changing the operative sense of “divided.”","context":"Hernandez shifts from division of a physical object's components to division of personhood into percentages or degrees."},{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-2","decision":"accepted","rationale":"“Divided” shifts from the partition of physical components to possessing personhood in percentages, so two different senses drive the nonidentity inference.","context":"He shifts from physical objects being divisible into parts to personhood not occurring in percentages or degrees."}]}
{"candidateKey":"pro-09|fallacy|Begging the question","kind":"card","definition":"Assuming, directly or indirectly, the very point that needs to be established, so the conclusion is smuggled into the premises.","source":{"moveId":"pro-09","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"unity, indivisibility, and identity through change","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"Because changing physical aggregates allegedly lose identity while persons remain the same subjects through change, Hernandez concludes that personal persistence requires an immaterial soul.","lockedProposition":"Unlike changing physical aggregates, the person persists as the same subject through change; therefore personal identity is grounded in an immaterial soul.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"moving on we have identity through change now aggregates do not retain identity change and we'll get to that in a second but the argument is I'm either a purely physical object or an immaterial soul if I am a purely physical object and I do not retain identity through change I do maintain identity to change therefore I'm not a purely physical object therefore I am a soul now if I'm nothing more than a","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: Hernandez isolates diachronic personal identity as the explanandum and states a direct inference: changing physical aggregates do not retain numerical identity, yet the person does, so the persisting subject is an immaterial soul. Principal limitation: The aggregate premise is asserted rather than established, because continuity might consist in preserved organization, causal history, bodily development, or overlapping psychological relations despite material replacement, leaving his physical-or-soul contrast underjustified. Live burden: He must specify a criterion of numerical identity that an organized organism cannot satisfy, then show how an immaterial subject secures sameness across change without merely transferring the identity question from body to soul."},"presentedReviews":[{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-1","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The asserted physical-object-or-soul choice excludes organized bodily, causal, and psychological continuity accounts in the premise, thereby assuming the ontological restriction needed for the soul conclusion.","context":"Hernandez assumes the persisting person must be either a purely physical object or an immaterial soul before excluding physical continuity accounts."},{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-2","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The physical-object-or-soul premise presupposes the exhaustive contrast required for the conclusion, excluding organized physical continuity at the outset rather than defeating it.","context":"He assumes one must be either a purely physical object lacking persistence or an immaterial soul before arguing for the soul."}]}
{"candidateKey":"con-10|fallacy|Begging the question","kind":"card","definition":"Assuming, directly or indirectly, the very point that needs to be established, so the conclusion is smuggled into the premises.","source":{"moveId":"con-10","side":"con","sectionTitle":"unity, indivisibility, and identity through change","role":"Reply","currentDescription":"Because mind emerges from an integrated brain, body, endocrine system, and history, Aron Ra concludes that transferring information into another body would not preserve the same person.","lockedProposition":"Mind emerges from the integrated brain and body, including endocrine influences, so transfer to a differently organized body would not preserve the same person as detachable data.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"mind is an emergent aspect of the body not just one part of the brain but the whole brain collectively and really the rest of the body - even your endocrine system influences some portion of who and what you are including your desires and aspects of your sexuality even if you could remove your mind and place it in someone else's body it wouldn't be you anymore because your mind is not","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: Aron Ra answers Hernandez’s particle-aggregate challenge with a concrete embodied alternative: personhood depends on integrated neural, bodily, endocrine, and experiential organization, so neither unchanged matter nor transferable mental information independently preserves the person. Principal limitation: Emergence identifies the relevant system but does not specify what makes that system numerically continuous through reorganization, while the differently embodied transfer example assumes the identity discontinuity it must establish. Live burden: Aron Ra must state a principled continuity condition that distinguishes development, endocrine change, impairment, gradual replacement, and wholesale transfer, then explain why integrated physical organization yields categorical person-status despite component divisibility."},"presentedReviews":[{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-1","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The hypothetical transfer is offered to illuminate personal identity, but the claim that the transferred mind would no longer be the person simply assumes the disputed identity discontinuity.","context":"Aron Ra says a mind placed in another body would no longer be the same person, assuming the identity discontinuity under dispute."},{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-2","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The body-transfer example asserts that the transferred mind would no longer be the person, even though that identity discontinuity is the point the example must establish.","context":"He says a mind placed in another body would no longer be you, assuming the disputed identity discontinuity."}]}
{"candidateKey":"con-15|bias|Subjective validation","kind":"card","definition":"Treating a claim as especially accurate or meaningful because it feels personally resonant or confirms one's private experience.","source":{"moveId":"con-15","side":"con","sectionTitle":"free will, rational inference, and mental causation","role":"Reply","currentDescription":"Because upbringing and past experience influence but do not prohibit evidence-responsive judgment, Aron Ra concludes that rational decisions need not originate from an uncaused immaterial chooser.","lockedProposition":"Past experience and upbringing shape openness and inclination, yet those influences do not prohibit right decisions or rational judgment.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"what makes us honest but you there's some point when you realize that when how open you are to this or whether you're inclined to make a give a decision really is based on your past experiences and who you are how you were raised and so I've got a lot of other things but it doesn't mean that I own strain prohibited from making the right decisions or from being rational in them","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: Aron Ra distinguishes causal influence from rational incapacity, acknowledging that history and character shape inclinations while maintaining that a person can still consider information, revise judgment, and make a better-supported decision. Principal limitation: The account remains largely introspective and does not fully explain how reasons acquire normative authority or how responsibility should be understood if every deliberative capacity has a prior causal history. Live burden: He must show only that soul-based origination is unnecessary, but that requires a sufficiently developed alternative connecting causal formation, sensitivity to evidence, and ownership of the resulting choice."},"presentedReviews":[{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-2","decision":"rejected","rationale":"The reply draws partly on introspection, but it does not treat a claim as accurate merely because it personally resonates; it advances a general distinction between causal influence and rational incapacity.","context":null}]}
{"candidateKey":"con-25|bias|Subjective validation","kind":"card","definition":"Treating a claim as especially accurate or meaningful because it feels personally resonant or confirms one's private experience.","source":{"moveId":"con-25","side":"con","sectionTitle":"free will, rational inference, and mental causation","role":"Reply","currentDescription":"Because new information can alter choices despite prior tendencies, Aron Ra concludes that causally influenced decision-making remains evidence-responsive and need not be fixed before deliberation occurs.","lockedProposition":"Past tendencies influence but do not predetermine acceptance or rejection of evidence before it is encountered; decisions remain responsive to new information.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"because we're not all my all my decisions are not determined before I have that data now why there are indications and tendencies and I will lean because of them because of these indications and past experiences everything I can still make a decision based on information it's not determined that I'm going to accept or reject this information before I even know what it","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: Aron Ra distinguishes dispositions shaped by prior experience from accepting or rejecting evidence before that evidence arrives, making his reply directly responsive to Hernandez while preserving information-sensitive deliberation amid substantial causal influence. Principal limitation: Because new information and the agent’s response may themselves be causally determined, the cited experience of deciding from evidence does not demonstrate metaphysical openness or establish the ultimate origination Hernandez demands. Live burden: Aron Ra must defend the narrower claim that accountability depends on an agent’s capacity to register reasons, revise judgment, and own the resulting decision, then explain why those capacities require neither freedom from antecedent causes nor a soul."},"presentedReviews":[{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-2","decision":"rejected","rationale":"The speaker references his experience of information-sensitive choice, but the assessment identifies an incomplete metaphysical inference rather than reliance on personal resonance as validation.","context":null}]}
{"candidateKey":"con-03|fallacy|Equivocation","kind":"card","definition":"Shifting the meaning of a key word or phrase during the argument, making the reasoning seem stronger than it is.","source":{"moveId":"con-03","side":"con","sectionTitle":"biological continuity, emergence, and explanatory need","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"Because modern biology explains living organization without an animating life force, Aron Ra concludes that a traditional biological role for the soul is explanatorily unnecessary.","lockedProposition":"Modern biology explains living bodies without the once-proposed animating life force, removing a traditional explanatory role for a soul.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"notion of living energy the theory of vitalism was disproved in 1828 there is no life force animating our bodies and our current understanding of biology in the 21st century shows that there was never need for such a thing our media","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: Aron Ra targets “animates” in Hernandez’s definition by invoking modern biology’s ability to explain metabolism, reproduction, regulation, and organismal activity without an additional life force, thereby removing a traditional explanatory task assigned to souls. Principal limitation: The compressed claim that vitalism was disproved does not distinguish that discarded biological force from Hernandez’s proposed consciousness-bearing substantial subject, so defeating the former neither directly tests nor refutes the latter. Live burden: Aron Ra must either confine his conclusion to biological animation or establish that Hernandez’s soul duplicates vitalism’s obsolete causal role instead of describing consciousness’s relation to a body already alive."},"presentedReviews":[{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-1","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The reply treats Hernandez's consciousness-bearing soul that “animates” a body as the discarded biological life force of vitalism, shifting between materially different senses of animation and soul.","context":"Aron Ra recasts Hernandez's consciousness-bearing animating soul as vitalism's biological life force and treats refuting the latter as answering the former."},{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-2","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Aron Ra shifts Hernandez’s “animates” from a consciousness-bearing soul’s relation to the body to vitalism’s biological life force, then treats refuting the latter as answering the former.","context":"He recasts the soul’s proposed animation of the body as vitalism’s obsolete biological life force."}]}
{"candidateKey":"pro-07|fallacy|Argument from ignorance","kind":"card","definition":"Treating a claim as true because it has not been disproved, or false because it has not been proved, rather than supplying positive evidence.","source":{"moveId":"pro-07","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"brain dependence, embodiment, animals, and emergence","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"Because dependence does not prove identity, Hernandez concludes that Aron Ra must demonstrate complete property identity between mind and brain before calling consciousness wholly physical.","lockedProposition":"Mind–brain dependence does not establish physicalism; identity would require mind and brain to share all properties in both directions.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"identity so if Aaron wants to show that consciousness is something physical he's going to have to do far more than show that there's a cause effect or dependency relationship between the mind and brain at the very least he's going to have to show that they necessarily share all the same properties that there nothing true the mind that is not sure the brain and vice versa in other words she's going to have to show identity and","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: Hernandez articulates a stringent bidirectional identity test, requiring every relevant property of mind to belong to brain and conversely, which precisely explains why observed neural dependence or correlation cannot deductively establish numerical identity. Principal limitation: This formulation makes Aron Ra prove the strongest physicalist conclusion, although he could answer the proposed soul by contesting its affirmative warrant without establishing reductive mind-brain identity. Live burden: Hernandez must supply independent grounds for a soul and show that apparent property differences concern the entities themselves, rather than mental and neural descriptions operating at distinct explanatory levels."},"presentedReviews":[{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-2","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Hernandez imposes an excessive burden on physicalism, but this excerpt does not explicitly treat the soul as true merely because complete mind-brain identity has not been proved.","context":null}]}
{"candidateKey":"pro-07|fallacy|Special pleading","kind":"card","definition":"Applying an exception or different standard to one side of the argument without giving a principled reason for the difference.","source":{"moveId":"pro-07","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"brain dependence, embodiment, animals, and emergence","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"Because dependence does not prove identity, Hernandez concludes that Aron Ra must demonstrate complete property identity between mind and brain before calling consciousness wholly physical.","lockedProposition":"Mind–brain dependence does not establish physicalism; identity would require mind and brain to share all properties in both directions.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"identity so if Aaron wants to show that consciousness is something physical he's going to have to do far more than show that there's a cause effect or dependency relationship between the mind and brain at the very least he's going to have to show that they necessarily share all the same properties that there nothing true the mind that is not sure the brain and vice versa in other words she's going to have to show identity and","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: Hernandez articulates a stringent bidirectional identity test, requiring every relevant property of mind to belong to brain and conversely, which precisely explains why observed neural dependence or correlation cannot deductively establish numerical identity. Principal limitation: This formulation makes Aron Ra prove the strongest physicalist conclusion, although he could answer the proposed soul by contesting its affirmative warrant without establishing reductive mind-brain identity. Live burden: Hernandez must supply independent grounds for a soul and show that apparent property differences concern the entities themselves, rather than mental and neural descriptions operating at distinct explanatory levels."},"presentedReviews":[{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-1","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Hernandez requires the negative to prove complete bidirectional mind-brain identity while treating the affirmative soul as viable without comparable positive establishment, applying an unprincipled asymmetry in burdens.","context":"Hernandez requires Aron Ra to prove complete mind-brain identity while offering no corresponding positive proof that an immaterial soul exists."}]}
{"candidateKey":"pro-13|fallacy|Appeal to authority","kind":"card","definition":"Relying on a person or institution's status as if it settles the argument, especially when the relevant evidence, expertise, or consensus has not been shown.","source":{"moveId":"pro-13","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"brain dependence, embodiment, animals, and emergence","role":"Reply","currentDescription":"Because damaging an instrument can impair its operation without harming its user, Hernandez concludes that brain damage is compatible with an embodied soul and does not establish identity.","lockedProposition":"Hernandez does not require present immortality; he argues that damaging the instrument used by the soul can impair operation without identifying the soul with that instrument.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"so I didn't argue about the soul being immortal that wasn't part of my argument but you're on the right track in that there is a cause and effect a correlation between that but correlations are not explanations as atheist philosopher was so human are you so if so what can be damages the instrument the soil uses I cannot damage the note C but I can damage a guitar and a piano I can damage the keys associated","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: Hernandez carefully narrows his claim by denying that present immortality is required, conceding causal correlation between brain condition and mental performance, and offering damaged guitars or pianos as a coherent model of impaired operation. Principal limitation: This analogy establishes bare compatibility rather than evidence for a soul, and instrument damage poorly parallels the highly specific changes to memory, personality, integration, and awareness following neural injury. Live burden: He must explain why distinct mental capacities track distinct neural systems so precisely, then derive those patterns prospectively from the instrument model instead of accommodating impairments retrospectively."},"presentedReviews":[{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-1","decision":"rejected","rationale":"The fragmentary mention of an atheist philosopher accompanies the claim that correlations are not explanations, but neither the assessment nor the excerpt makes that authority a material substitute for the instrument analogy's reasoning.","context":null}]}
{"candidateKey":"pro-15|fallacy|Appeal to authority","kind":"card","definition":"Relying on a person or institution's status as if it settles the argument, especially when the relevant evidence, expertise, or consensus has not been shown.","source":{"moveId":"pro-15","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"empirical testing, near-death experiences, and paranormal claims","role":"Reply","currentDescription":"Hernandez claims some near-death reports contain verified inaccessible knowledge but concludes that they should remain secondary because he has not studied them sufficiently to defend them.","lockedProposition":"Hernandez believes some near-death reports are verified and evidential but concedes he has not studied them enough to present and defend them.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"think there's a lot of verified in des and there were some there even published by from ups like in scientific journals and and reputable people in places that have been verified by doctors and there's a lot of lot of interesting cases where people know things that could not have known where I mean III can go through a lot but it's something I haven't studied to the satisfaction of being willing to present and defend it I","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: Hernandez responsibly distinguishes his principal philosophical case from paranormal material he cannot adequately defend, explicitly acknowledging insufficient study rather than using vaguely remembered reports as decisive proof of a separable soul. Principal limitation: His references to numerous verified cases, doctors, reputable investigators, and scientific journals remain wholly nonspecific, leaving the meaning of verification and the claimed inaccessible knowledge unavailable for evaluation. Live burden: If he wishes these reports to reinforce the affirmative case, he must present representative cases, verification procedures, alternative explanations, and a clear bridge from anomalous knowledge to an immaterial substantial subject."},"presentedReviews":[{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-1","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The claimed verification is supported only by the status of scientific journals, reputable people, and doctors; no cases or verification evidence are supplied for evaluation.","context":"Hernandez supports alleged verified near-death cases by invoking scientific journals, reputable people, and doctors without identifying evidence or verification procedures."},{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-2","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Doctors, reputable people, and journals are mentioned vaguely, but Hernandez expressly declines to present the reports as a defended argument; the citation does not function as settling proof.","context":null}]}
{"candidateKey":"con-22|fallacy|Appeal to authority","kind":"card","definition":"Relying on a person or institution's status as if it settles the argument, especially when the relevant evidence, expertise, or consensus has not been shown.","source":{"moveId":"con-22","side":"con","sectionTitle":"empirical testing, near-death experiences, and paranormal claims","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"Because competing research and brain stimulation reproduce relevant effects, Aron Ra concludes that out-of-body reports do not provide secure evidence of literal separation from the body.","lockedProposition":"Competing papers and brain-stimulation replication undermine the use of out-of-body reports as evidence that subjects literally leave their bodies.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"it's a peer-reviewed paper but as I said there are other papers that show contradicting statements and then there's the god helmet that duplicates all of these effects so the people who","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: Aron Ra offers two checks on the inference from out-of-body experiences to bodily departure: peer-reviewed findings conflict, and brain stimulation through the god helmet reportedly reproduces the effects. Principal limitation: He provides no studies, methods, controls, effect frequencies, or comparisons of evidential quality, while saying the helmet duplicates all effects exceeds the unspecific detail offered and obscures differences among experiences. Live burden: Aron Ra must specify which sensations or observations were reproduced, establish how closely they match the reports at issue, and explain how the competing research should reduce confidence without claiming to disprove every experience."},"presentedReviews":[{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-1","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Mentioning peer review gives the unidentified paper some status, but the claim is not treated as settled by that status: the speaker expressly notes conflicting papers and separately invokes experimental replication. The defect assessed is inadequate sourcing and comparison.","context":null},{"anonymousReviewer":"candidate-2","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Calling a paper peer-reviewed is a citation-quality claim, but Aron Ra also alleges conflicting findings and reproduced effects rather than treating publication status alone as decisive.","context":null}]}
